<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_7-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Comparing two groups: Quantitative response</title>

<p>A worksheet for Grace College from Zybooks</p>

<subsection> 
 <title>Cancer Pamphlets</title>
<p>
Researchers in Philadelphia investigated whether pamphlets containing informa- tion for cancer patients 
are written at a level that the cancer patients can compre- hend 
(<url href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10691898.1995.11910492">Short, Moriarty, and Cooley, 1995
</url>). First, they measured the readability of a sample of cancer pamphlets based on factors 
such as the length of sentences and the number of polysyllabic words, assigning each pamphlet a 
grade level. The results shown below are presented as a frequency table, reporting the number of 
pamphlets at each grade level.
</p>

 
<table>
<title>Readability measures (grade level) for pamphlets aimed at cancer patients.</title>
<tabular><row >
<cell>Pamphlets' readability levels</cell>
<cell>6</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>11</cell>
<cell>12</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>16</cell>
</row>
<row >
<cell>Count (number of pamphlets)</cell>
<cell>3</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell>
<cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell>
</row>
</tabular></table>

<activity>
 <!--Question 1-->
<statement>
<p>What are the observational units in the table above? How many observational units were measured?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Observational units are the pamphlets. 30
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 2-->
<statement>
<p>Use the information in the table above to construct a histogram of the pamphlets' readability 
levels. How would you summarize the behavior of this histogram?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The histogram is slightly skewed to the right.
  </p>
  <image source="7-1/a-7-1-2.png" width="50%">
    <description>Histogram of reading level with largest mode at 8, second-largest mode at 12, and a slight right tail.</description>
  </image>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 3-->
<statement>
<p>Using the information in the table above, calculate the mean pamphlet readability level. 
(Hint: Add up the values, 6 + 6 + 6 + 7 + … + 15 + 15 + 16 and divide by the number of 
observational units.)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Mean reading level of pamphlets is 9.8.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 4-->
<statement>
<p>Using the information in the table above, calculate the median pamphlet readability 
level. How many observations are on each side of this median value (including the repeat 
values)? (Hint: What is the position of the median?)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Median reading level of pamphlets is 9. There are 15 observations on each side of the median.     The median is half-way between the 15th and 16th highest values (position 15.5), both of which are 9.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 5-->
<statement>
<p>How do the mean and median compare? Is this what you would have predicted from 
the histogram? Explain briefly.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The mean is slightly larger than the median. Yes, with a skewed distribution the mean is pulled toward the tail.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>
While the mean and median tell us about the center of the distribution, one way to 
summarize the behavior of the distribution of a quantitative variable is by 
dividing the distribution into four pieces of roughly equal size (number of 
observations). In other words, summarize the distribution by determining where 
the bottom 25% of the data are, the next 25%, the next 25%, and then the top 25%.
</p>

<definition>
 <statement>
The value for which 25% of the data lie below that value is called the <term>lower quartile</term> 
(or 25th percentile). Similarly, the value for which 25% of the data lie above that 
value is called the <term>upper quartile</term> (or 75th percentile). Quartiles can be calculated 
by determining the median of the values above/below the location of the overall median. 
The difference between the quartiles is called the <term>inter-quartile range (IQR)</term>, another 
measure of variability along with standard deviation.
</statement>
</definition>

<activity>
 <!--Question 6-->
<statement>
<p>Take the 15 values below the position of the median, and find the median of those 
15 values. This is the lower quartile.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Lower quartile, <m>Q_1=8</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 7-->
<statement>
<p>Repeat for the 15 values above the position of the median. This is the upper quartile.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Upper quartile, <m>Q_3=12</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 8-->
<statement>
<p>Calculate and interpret the inter-quartile range.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Inter-quartile range, <m>Q_3-Q_1 = 4</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 9-->
<statement>
<p>Explain why the inter-quartile range might be preferred to the standard deviation to 
summarize the variability in the pamphlet reading levels.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Because the distribution of reading levels is skewed, the standard deviation may be a bit     misleading as it assume the variability below the mean is the same (on average) as the amount of variability above the mean .The inter-quartile range is not affected by outliers.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <p>
    The minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, and maximum comprise the 
    <term>five-number summary</term>. A visual representation of the five-number 
    summary is a boxplot. The figure below shows a boxplot for these data.
  </p>
</remark>

<figure> 
 <caption> Boxplot of cancer pamphlet reading levels </caption>
<image source="7-1/boxplot.png" width="100%">
<description>Boxplot of cancer pamphlet reading levels.
</description>
</image>
</figure>

<definition>
 <statement>
A <term>boxplot</term> is a visual display of the five-number summary. The 
box displays the middle 50% of the distribution, and its width (the IQR) 
helps us visualize the spread of the distribution; the whiskers extend to 
the smallest and largest values in the dataset.
</statement>
</definition>

<activity>
 <!--Question 10-->
<statement>
<p>How does the boxplot match up to the five-number summary? (Endpoint of 
lower whisker, lower edge of box, line within the box, upper edge of box, 
upper whisker).</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The boxplot construction matches up to the 5-number summary: endpoint of lower whisker is min = 6, lower edge of box is <m>Q_1=8</m>, line within box is median = 9, upper edge of box is <m>Q_3=12</m>, endpoint of upper whisker is max = 16.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>
The boxplot illustrates that there is some variability in the pamphlet 
reading levels. What the researchers wanted to know was how these 
different reading levels matched up to the reading ability of the cancer 
patients. The table below shows a frequency table for the reading level 
(again a grade level) for 63 cancer patients. Note that patient reading 
levels of under 3rd grade and above 12th grade are not determined exactly.
</p>
 
<table>
<title> Comparing pamphlet readability scores and patient reading levels </title>
<tabular><row header="yes">
<cell>Patients' reading levels</cell>
<cell>&lt;3</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>11</cell>
<cell>12</cell><cell>&gt;12</cell><cell>Total</cell>
</row>
<row header="yes">
<cell>Count (number of patients)</cell>
<cell>6</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>2</cell>
<cell>6</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>17</cell><cell>63</cell>
</row>
</tabular></table>

<activity>
 <!--Question 11-->
<statement>
<p>Explain why it is not possible to calculate the mean reading ability for these patients.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The exact reading level for those less than 3 and more than 12 is not known, so 23 of the 63 patients don't have an exact reading level.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 12-->
<statement>
<p>Explain why it is possible to calculate the median reading ability for 
these patients and do so.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The median is the 50th percentile, because calculating the median only involves the position of the (ordered) observations, we don't need the exact numerical values of the other observations. Median reading level of patients is 9.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 13-->
<statement>
<p>How does the median reading level of the patients compare to the median 
reading level of the pamphlets? Does this indicate that the pamphlets are a 
good match to the cancer patients? Explain.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The median reading level of the patients is exactly the same as the median reading level of the pamphlets; 9. Not necessarily, the spread of reading levels needs to be considered, as does the shape of the distribution of reading levels for both the pamphlets and the patients.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 14-->
<statement>
<p>Determine and interpret the lower quartile for the patient reading levels.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    <m>Q_1</m> for the cancer patients is 5. Twenty five percent of cancer patients have reading levels below grade 5.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="q-7-1-15">
 <!--Question 15-->
<statement>
<p>How does the lower quartile of patient reading levels compare to the lower 
quartile of the reading level of the cancer pamphlets? How does the lower 
quartile of patient reading levels compare to the minimum reading level of 
the cancer pamphlets?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The lower quartile for the reading level of the patients is 5, which is 3 grades lower than the lower quartile for the reading level of the cancer pamphlets (8). The lower quartile for the reading level of the patients is 5, which is 1 grade lower than the minimum reading level for the cancer pamphlets (6).
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 16-->
<statement>
<p>Use your answer to question <xref ref="q-7-1-15"/> to decide whether the cancer pamphlets 
are a good match to patient reading levels. (Hint: Interpret the second 
comparison in <xref ref="q-7-1-15"/> in context.)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The reading levels of the pamphlets are not a good match to the reading levels of the patients; 17/63 (27%) of the cancer patients can't read even the simplest pamphlet. Pamphlets need to err on the side of being readable to all patients.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>
Later in this chapter, you will learn formal methods for assessing whether 
the centers of two distributions are statistically significantly different. 
But notice how such analysis may not be valid for a study like this: 
<ul>
<li>Means are not the most important feature to be comparing. For example,
 means will be influenced by outliers, and these data were not presented 
 in a way to allow for calculation of means. </li>
<li>We could focus on comparing the medians instead, but only 
comparing the centers of these distributions ignores the 
variability in the distributions, which is perhaps of more 
interest to these research questions. 
</li>
<li>We don't know whether the pamphlets or patients are randomly 
sampled from larger populations. </li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>This exploration reveals that measures of center do not always 
tell the whole story when you are analyzing data to address a 
particular research question. In this case, the research question 
of whether pamphlets' readability levels are well aligned with 
patients' reading levels requires looking at the entire distributions, 
not simply at measures of center. Examining displays of the distributions 
would be a better place to begin. In addition to dotplots and histograms, 
boxplots are another potential type of display that is especially useful 
for comparing distributions. But keep in mind, as shown in a previous 
exploration, that they can sometimes mask important features of 
distributions. Many software packages now allow you to overlay a 
boxplot on top of a histogram or dotplot to help see the entire 
distribution while highlighting key features like the quartiles.
</p>
</subsection>

</section>